        "thinking": "Now I have a clear picture of the auth middleware. It handles:\n1. `local_trusted` mode: auto-board\n2. `authenticated` mode with session (BetterAuth session resolution)\n3. Board key (`Authorization: Bearer \u003cboard_key\u003e`)\n4. Agent JWT (`Authorization: Bearer \u003cjwt\u003e`)\n5. Agent API key (`Authorization: Bearer \u003cagent_api_key\u003e`)\n\nThe auth logic uses the DB directly, so it will work fine with Hyperdrive in the CF Worker.\n\nNow let me plan the complete implementation. I'll use a subagent to build the CF-specific pieces since there's a lot to implement. \n\nBefore diving in, I need to think through the architecture. The key challenge is converting Express handlers to Hono—I can either extract the original handler from the Express middleware wrapper or rebuild them for Hono. I'll tag the Express middleware with a reference to the original handler, then walk the router stacks to extract it. This way I can reuse the existing handler logic without rewriting it.\n\nMy plan is to modify the Express adapter to attach the handler as metadata, then create a bridge module that walks the Express router's internal layer structure to reconstruct route definitions for Hono. The bridge will need to handle both direct routes and nested routers, extracting the path from regex patterns and collecting all the tagged handlers for each route.\n\nExtracting paths from router layers... Writing route extraction logic...\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a subtlety with how the route factories are mounted in the app—they're being registered with `api.use()`, so I need to make sure the nested router extraction handles that correctly.\n\nLooking at the actual route files, I see they're using absolute paths like `/companies/:id/...` and `/companies/:companyId/goals`, which means they're already fully qualified even when mounted under a prefix. So the prefix handling in the extraction logic might be redundant or could cause double-pathing issues.",
